{"groups":[{"members":["c4","c1"],"primary":"c4","title":"California FEHA and Civil Rights Council limits on criminal-history screening of rental applicants","requirement":"Housing providers may check criminal history, but may not use blanket bans on applicants with a criminal record or consider arrests without conviction, sealed or expunged records, or juvenile records. A denial based on a conviction must be directly related to tenancy and safety concerns, weighing the nature and severity of the crime and the time elapsed.","key_value":"No blanket criminal-history bans; arrests without conviction, sealed/expunged and juvenile records may not be considered","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2020-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA); California Civil Rights Council (formerly Fair Employment and Housing Council) regulations on use of criminal history in housing","coverage_conditions":"Applies to most housing providers and housing-related services, including landlords, tenant screening companies and property managers. Covered housing includes single-family homes, multi-family homes, condominiums, apartments, mobile homes and subsidized housing.","exemptions":"The official source says an owner who occupies a home and rents to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics, but not through discriminatory advertising. Senior housing may be limited by age. The source does not say how these exceptions apply to criminal-history screening.","coverage_from":"c4","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"SB 329: source of income includes government housing subsidies (\"No Section 8\" policies prohibited)","requirement":"SB 329 expanded FEHA's definition of \"source of income\" to include government housing subsidies such as Section 8. Landlords may not refuse to rent to applicants or decline participation because of such subsidies, so \"No Section 8\" policies are unlawful.","key_value":"Refusing Section 8 / government housing subsidy participation is prohibited","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2020-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"SB 329 (2019), amending the Fair Employment and Housing Act (Cal. Gov. Code § 12955)","coverage_conditions":"All landlords subject to FEHA; no building-level coverage limits stated.","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c2","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c5","c3"],"primary":"c5","title":"SB 267: credit history and income standards for applicants with government rent subsidies","requirement":"Where an applicant has a government rent subsidy, an owner may not use an income standard that is not based on the tenant's portion of the rent. The owner also may not use credit history unless it offers the applicant the option to provide lawful, verifiable alternative evidence of ability to pay that portion. If the applicant provides such evidence, the owner must give reasonable time to respond and reasonably consider it instead of credit history.","key_value":"Must offer alternative evidence of ability to pay the tenant's portion of rent when using credit history for subsidized applicants","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 12955 (as amended by SB 267)","coverage_conditions":"Applies to owners of housing accommodations where the applicant has a government rent subsidy (e.g., Section 8 or HUD-VASH vouchers); no building-size or age limits stated.","exemptions":"Owners may still ask about the level or source of income, request employment verification and landlord references, and verify identity.","coverage_from":"c5","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
